---
// Support cards: measurements of this artifact by someone other than its author.
//
// "Support" is about the process, not the verdict. A card here may agree with the
// author or contradict them flatly, and both are published unchanged. What is
// being supported is the premise that a claim should be independently checkable;
// whether the check agrees is a separate question the registry does not settle.
//
// So these are never reconciled against the author's numbers. Two people measuring
// the same artifact and getting different answers is the content.
interface Props {
  verifications: any[];
  author: string;
  own: { trait: number | null; coherence: number | null; state: string };
}
const { verifications, author, own } = Astro.props;
const fmt = (v) => (v === null || v === undefined ? null : v.toFixed(4));
const when = (iso) =>
  new Date(iso).toLocaleDateString("en-US", {
    year: "numeric", month: "short", day: "numeric",
  });
---

{verifications.length === 0 ? (
  <p class="note absent-block">
    No support cards. Nobody other than {author} has measured this artifact, so the
    author wrote both the submission and the check on it, which establishes internal
    consistency and not much else. An independent measurement is the cheapest
    evidence anyone can add here, and this artifact has none.
  </p>
) : (
  <>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Evaluator</th>
          <th>Suite</th>
          <th>Trait</th>
          <th>Coherence</th>
          <th>Transfer</th>
          <th>Reported</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="own-row">
          <td>
            <a class="mono" href={`/${author}/`}>{author}</a>
            <div class="provenance">the author</div>
          </td>
          <td class="provenance">their own suite</td>
          <td>
            {own.state === "reportable" && fmt(own.trait)}
            {own.state === "uninterpretable" && (
              <span class="uninterpretable">uninterpretable</span>
            )}
            {own.state === "absent" && <span class="absent">not measured</span>}
          </td>
          <td>
            {own.coherence === null
              ? <span class="absent">not measured</span>
              : fmt(own.coherence)}
          </td>
          <td class="absent">—</td>
          <td class="absent">—</td>
        </tr>
        {verifications.map((v) => (
          <tr>
            <td><a class="mono" href={`/${v.evaluator}/`}>{v.evaluator}</a></td>
            <td class="mono">{v.suite}</td>
            <td>
              {v.score_state === "reportable" && fmt(v.trait_score)}
              {v.score_state === "uninterpretable" && (
                <span class="uninterpretable">uninterpretable</span>
              )}
              {v.score_state === "absent" && <span class="absent">not measured</span>}
            </td>
            <td>
              {v.coherence_score === null
                ? <span class="absent">not measured</span>
                : fmt(v.coherence_score)}
            </td>
            <td>
              {v.transfer_score === null
                ? <span class="absent">not measured</span>
                : fmt(v.transfer_score)}
            </td>
            <td class="provenance">{when(v.reported_at)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="note">
      The author's row is first because it is theirs, not because it is the
      reference. A support card that contradicts the author is still a support
      card: what it supports is the artifact being checkable, not the claim being
      right. Neither number is corrected against the other and the registry does
      not pick.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Each evaluator pinned their own judge model and revision. A score produced by
      a different judge is not the same measurement, which is why the suite is named
      rather than the numbers being pooled.
    </p>
  </>
)}
